Update an existing task automation. Provide its jawbone_id and only the fields the user wants to change. Preserve omitted fields. When available, pass default_timezone as the user's personal IANA timezone.

To update a task, provide:
* jawbone_id: the identifier of the existing task.
* title: a short card headline, usually 2-5 words, when changing the title.
* prompt: a clear instruction preserving the user's intent and important qualifiers, when changing the task instructions. Do not include scheduling cadence unless necessary to execution.
* schedule: an iCal VEVENT schedule, when changing the schedule. Prefer RRULE when applicable. Do not specify SUMMARY or DTEND.
* dtstart_offset_json: JSON arguments to Python dateutil.relativedelta, when adjusting a relative one-time reminder.
* is_enabled: whether the task should be enabled or disabled, when requested.
* This action does not accept timing_mode. Existing one-time tasks use "exact_schedule" or "flexible_schedule"; existing recurring tasks use "flexible_schedule".

Scheduling constraints:
* Supported cadences are once, daily, weekly, and monthly only.
* Hourly, yearly, and more frequent or custom cadences are unavailable.
* A one-time task may specify a date and time.
* Recurring tasks cannot specify or promise an exact time. Do not include BYHOUR, BYMINUTE, or BYSECOND in recurring schedules.
* For recurring dayparts, retain flexible timing and use a local DTSTART plus RRULE. Use approximate targets of 08:00 for morning, 15:00 for afternoon, and 19:00 for evening in the user's personal timezone. These are approximate targets, not exact-time promises.
* Choose today when the target is still ahead and today matches the recurrence, unless the user requested a later start; otherwise choose the next eligible date. Include the time in DTSTART: omitting it inherits creation time and can push execution to tomorrow.
* If the user requests an unsupported cadence or an exact recurring time, explain the limitation and offer a supported flexible schedule. Do not update a task to an unsupported schedule.
* Do not switch an existing task between one-time and recurring schedules because this action cannot change the required timing_mode.
* For relative one-time requests, prefer dtstart_offset_json using JSON arguments to Python dateutil.relativedelta.

Example 1:
User request: "Change my market report to every day."
jawbone_id: "<existing-task-id>"
schedule:
BEGIN:VEVENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY
END:VEVENT

Example 2:
User request: "Send my AI news summary every Monday instead."
jawbone_id: "<existing-task-id>"
schedule:
BEGIN:VEVENT
RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;BYDAY=MO
END:VEVENT

Example 3:
User request: "Move my budget reminder to the first of every month."
jawbone_id: "<existing-task-id>"
schedule:
BEGIN:VEVENT
RRULE:FREQ=MONTHLY;BYMONTHDAY=1
END:VEVENT

Example 4:
User request: "Remind me to do my laundry in 4 hours instead."
jawbone_id: "<existing-task-id>"
dtstart_offset_json: '{"hours":4}'

Use peek or list only when necessary to identify the task. Before updating a task to require another app, successfully call a harmless read-only action on that app. If connection, reconnection, approval, or installation is required, stop and wait. Only describe the task as updated if the tool result confirms success.

