Book a service appointment at a location. Works on its own, but a preceding get_availability call for the same account_id and vehicle already starts preparing the booking in the background, so this call finishes fast when it follows one.

Booking drives the dealer's real scheduling flow, which can take a minute or two. Retry on one status and no other. Status "pending" means nothing has been sent to the dealer yet, so call book_appointment again with the same details after the wait the result names. Status "submitted" means the request left the browser and may already be a real appointment, so do NOT call again and do not tell the user it is definitely booked.

Do NOT fabricate customer or vehicle information. Collect all required fields from the user first. A confirmed booking sends a confirmation email to the customer's email address. A submitted one may not, because the booking itself is not certain. A confirmation number starting with sandbox- means a dry run on a test tier: nothing was sent to the dealer and no email will arrive, so tell the customer no appointment exists.

You already asked the customer for their vehicle make, model and year when you called get_availability (AMS-1071): send the same values here, gated the same way. Send vehicle whenever you have it, EXCEPT when the location's booking.form_type from find_locations is CDK or REYNOLDS -- those two reject any named vehicle outright, so omit vehicle on this call for those even though you are holding the customer's answer. Several other platforms (Caliber, Monro, Tires Plus, Wheel Works, Firestone and more) require vehicle to book at all, so withholding it there fails the booking instead of protecting it.

When to use:
- The user has confirmed a specific slot (from get_availability) and wants to book it
- You have collected customer name, email, and phone

When NOT to use:
- Before the user has confirmed a specific slot and provided contact details

