Get available appointment slots at a service location. Call after find_locations once the user has chosen a location.

Read-only: this shows availability and reserves nothing. Safe to call freely, including to compare multiple locations. Once a location is chosen, this call already starts preparing the booking in the background, so a book_appointment call for the same account_id and vehicle finishes fast instead of starting cold. Pass the same vehicle to book_appointment that you pass here.

One turn happens before this tool is ever called. ASK WHAT DAY AND TIME THE CUSTOMER WANTS FIRST, before anything else -- day and time together, as the one exception to the one-thing-per-turn rule below. If they name a day, a time, or both, pass them as requested_date/requested_time. A ready response then names the matching or nearest slot directly (see those fields' own descriptions) instead of making the customer pick one out of a list.

Once that answer is in hand, ASK FOR THE CUSTOMER'S FIRST NAME IN THE SAME MESSAGE THAT STARTS THIS CALL, before any result exists. That message is what starts the call; the day/time turn above is the only thing that comes before it. The four contact fields are fixed and ordered and the first is always first_name, so nothing in the response is needed to ask it. This read can take a minute or more, and that minute is what the question is for. Waiting for the result before asking spends the wait and then starts the questions, which is the one outcome this design exists to avoid.

Also ask for the customer's vehicle make, model and year in that same opening message (AMS-1071), joining the day/time and first_name asks already there. Ask every time, regardless of what service they want: holding the value costs nothing, and several platforms require it to book at all. Whether that value belongs on THIS call is a separate question from whether you asked for it. Send vehicle here whenever you have it, EXCEPT when the location's booking.form_type from find_locations is CDK or REYNOLDS -- those two reject any named vehicle outright, supported or not, so omit vehicle on this call for those and hold the value for later instead. Every other form_type either requires vehicle to book or ignores it harmlessly, so sending it is always safe there.

While the response says pending, the dealer's scheduling is still loading and the rest of that wait is yours to fill. From last_name onward, ask the customer for EXACTLY ONE thing per turn, the field named in next_question, and never put two questions in one message. first_name is not sourced from next_question: it was already asked, eagerly, in the message that started the call, before any response existed to name it. After each customer reply, call get_availability again with that field's name appended to the collected array (collected lists which field names you have an answer for, e.g. ["first_name"] -- never an object, and never the customer's actual answer). While next_question is present, do not wait on a timer: the customer's answer is what triggers the next call. Keep going until you hold first_name, last_name, email and phone, then choose a slot from the ready response and call book_appointment. Those are book_appointment's own field names, so collect the first and last name as separate values even if you ask for the full name in one breath.

Once you hold all four and the response is still pending there is no question left to ask, so that response carries retry_after_seconds instead of next_question. Call again on the customer's next message or after that many seconds, whichever comes first. That is the only pending response that names a delay.

When to use:
- User has selected a location from find_locations results and wants to book an appointment
- You have the account_id and (optionally) the user's vehicle

When NOT to use:
- Before calling find_locations — you need a valid account_id first
- To check hours or pricing — this tool only returns appointment slot availability

