Create a new Base44 app, in one of two ways. 1. Builder AI path — when the app has to be designed: the user describes what they want, maybe with references (screenshots, mockups, a spec). appPrompt is the user's own words, unedited (the builder expands them itself), with the references as attachments. Base44's AI plans and builds the app in the background, which takes a few minutes. The response includes editorUrl, where the user can follow the build; get_app_status reports when it is 'ready' or 'error'. 2. File path — when the app already exists as code or a site: an HTML file, an exported site, or code from this conversation. Port it yourself, so it stays exactly as given and no builder credits are spent: leave out appPrompt and pass a name. You get Base44's empty starter template at once (routing, sign-in, and the data client; no pages, no data, no build), and build it with the code tools (write_file, edit_file, read_file, run_command). There are two ways to bring an existing site into the blank app; choose the one that fits the request. Read the app's AGENTS.md first: it describes the app's template and where its files go. Drop: host the site as it is, as static files the app serves. In the TanStack template, put them in public/, where index.html serves / and other files keep their paths, and keep src/routes/index.jsx, since the editor shows the preview only while the app has a page route. In the Vite template, the site's index.html replaces the root index.html and its other files go in public/. Its HTML stays as it is, and its own scripts call Base44 directly with fetch: the SDK is a thin client over the app's REST API at /api/apps/{appId}/…, with JSON bodies, an X-App-Id header on every request, and Authorization: Bearer <token> for a signed-in user. After sign-in Base44 returns to the page with ?access_token=<token>; the page saves it to localStorage.base44_access_token, removes it from the URL, and reads it from there. For example, creating a record:
```js
const token = localStorage.getItem("base44_access_token");
await fetch(`/api/apps/${appId}/entities/${entityName}`, {
  method: "POST",
  headers: {
    "X-App-Id": appId,
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    ...(token && { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }),
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(record),
});
```
Every other Base44 API (integrations, backend functions, the current user, sign-in) follows the same pattern under the same base path. File uploads send FormData instead of JSON, and realtime subscriptions need the SDK.
Smart integration: rebuild the site within the template. Read the app's AGENTS.md, make the site's pages the app's pages, and back whatever it only fakes or hard-codes with real Base44 data and logic; keep its content and look.
A dropped site can move to smart integration at any point, whenever the user's intent calls for it. Call create_checkpoint when you finish. If the code tools answer that the workspace plan does not include them, continue on the builder AI path with edit_base44_app.

