Update entity records matching a query using MongoDB update operators. Does not require individual record IDs — use a query filter to match records and apply operators to all matches (up to 500 per call). IMPORTANT: The 'data' field MUST use update operators — bare field assignment like {"status": "active"} is not accepted; use {"$set": {"status": "active"}} instead. The built-in 'User' entity cannot be updated through this tool. If the response contains has_more=true, more records matched than the batch limit — call again with an adjusted query that excludes already-updated records (e.g. filter on the old value) to avoid an infinite loop. Supported operators: $set, $rename, $unset, $inc, $mul, $min, $max, $currentDate, $addToSet, $push, $pull. Examples: query={"status": "pending"}, data={"$set": {"status": "active"}} to update matching records; query={"category": "legacy"}, data={"$rename": {"oldField": "newField"}} to rename a field on matching records.

```ts
mcp__Base44__update_entities(args: // UpdateEntitiesInput
//
// Input schema for query-based bulk update using MongoDB operators.
{
// Appid
//
// The ID of the app containing the records
appId: string,
// Data
//
// MongoDB update operators to apply. Supported operators: $set, $rename, $unset, $inc, $mul, $min, $max, $currentDate, $addToSet, $push, $pull. Examples: {"$set": {"status": "active"}}, {"$rename": {"oldField": "newField"}}, {"$inc": {"count": 1}}, {"$unset": {"obsoleteField": ""}}.
data: { [key: string]: any },
// Entityname
//
// The entity name of the records (e.g. 'Invoice', 'Customer')
entityName: string, // maxLength: 63, pattern: /^\w+$/
// Query
//
// MongoDB-style filter to select which records to update. Examples: {"status": "active"}, {"amount": {"$gt": 100}}.
query: { [key: string]: any },
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Update the field definitions, types, or constraints of an existing entity. To modify data records, use update_entities. Always check existing schemas with list_entity_schemas first.

This also writes the entity's definition into the app's source code (base44/entities/<Name>.jsonc), so the change is durable.

IMPORTANT: Security rules are automatically preserved when omitted from your update:
- Entity-level RLS (the top-level 'rls' field) is preserved if you omit it.
- Field-level security (per-field 'rls' inside properties) is preserved for each field that omits it.
To intentionally modify or remove security rules, include them explicitly (use an empty object like 'rls': {} to clear).

## Row-Level Security (RLS)
Entity schemas can include RLS rules under the 'rls' field to restrict record access based on user identity. RLS rules are defined per operation: create, read, update, delete.

Template variables: {{user.id}}, {{user.email}}, {{user.role}}, {{user.data.<field>}}.

Rule types:
1. user_condition — exact equality check on user attributes (no MongoDB operators). E.g. {"user_condition": {"role": "admin"}}
2. Entity-user field comparison — MongoDB query syntax. Custom fields MUST use "data." prefix (e.g. "data.user_id": "{{user.id}}"). Built-in fields (id, created_by_id, created_date, updated_date) are at root level.

Combine with $and/$or/$in/$nin. Rules must reference user template variables, never static values.

Example: {"rls": {"read": {"created_by_id": "{{user.id}}"}, "update": {"$or": [{"created_by_id": "{{user.id}}"}, {"user_condition": {"role": "admin"}}]}}}

